WEBVTT

00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:05.102
Normalization helps reduce repeated facts and the anomalies they can create.

00:00:05.102 --> 00:00:09.442
An update anomaly occurs when the same fact appears in many rows and the copies become inconsistent.

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:17.772
An insertion anomaly occurs when you cannot store one fact without inventing another unrelated fact.

00:00:17.772 --> 00:00:22.932
A deletion anomaly occurs when removing one record accidentally removes the only copy of some other important

00:00:22.932 --> 00:00:28.692
information. The general goal is to store each fact in a place that matches what determines that fact.

00:00:28.692 --> 00:00:33.052
Normalization is not about splitting tables into as many pieces as possible.

00:00:33.052 --> 00:00:35.757
It is about making dependencies clearer so updates, inserts,

00:00:35.757 --> 00:00:40.307
and deletes do not accidentally damage unrelated information.

00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:43.512
Normalization is easier to understand as anomaly prevention.

00:00:43.512 --> 00:00:47.912
Repeating facts in many rows makes updates inconsistent, inserts awkward, and deletes risky.

00:00:47.912 --> 00:00:52.492
Splitting data into well-defined entities reduces that repetition.

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:59.120
The goal is not to create the maximum number of tables; it is to store each fact in a defensible place.
